---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import AbliteratedHubTable from '../components/AbliteratedHubTable.astro';
import { abliteratedGuide } from '../lib/status';
import { testedPickLabel } from '../lib/abliterated';

const testedPick = testedPickLabel();
---

<Base
  title="Abliterated models"
  description="What abliteration is versus uncensored fine-tunes, how to vet files, and the catalog we actually list — educational only. Q4 and up. Test the official instruct first."
>
  <main id="main" class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-10 sm:px-6">
    <p class="font-mono text-[0.72rem] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-accent">Weights · educational</p>
    <h1 class="mt-3 font-serif text-4xl tracking-tight sm:text-5xl">Abliterated models</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-muted">
      <strong class="font-medium text-ink">Abliterated</strong> means the refusals were edited in the weights so a local
      open model is more likely to do the task you asked — on your machine, with no hosted safety API in the middle.
      This hub is technical and adult. We do not host weights. You are responsible for what you run.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 rounded-2xl border border-ok/40 bg-ok/10 px-4 py-3 text-sm text-ok">
      Default stays official instruct — Qwen3.6-35B-A3B on llama.cpp. An abliterated checkpoint is a second model after
      that path is proven. Tested abliterated pick on our rig: <strong>{testedPick}</strong>.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-2 text-sm">
      <a class="rounded-full border border-line bg-raised px-4 py-2" href="/tutorials/run-local-llm">Run official instruct first</a>
      <a class="rounded-full border border-line bg-raised px-4 py-2" href="/calculator?style=abliterated">Open in the calculator</a>
      <a class="rounded-full border border-line bg-raised px-4 py-2" href="/budget?style=abliterated">Setup by budget</a>
    </p>

    <section class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl">Abliteration vs “uncensored”</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted">
        A Hugging Face title that says “uncensored” can mean a system-prompt jailbreak, a small SFT, or a real weight
        edit. Read the card. Abliteration (community “remove-refusals-with-transformers”, Heretic-style) subtracts or
        orthogonalizes a refusal direction in activations. There is no full retrain. An uncensored fine-tune / LoRA
        trains on completions that fulfill where the base refused. A jailbreak in the chat template is not a weight
        edit and is easy to undo.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted">
        Quality tradeoffs are real. Abliteration can cause think-loops, broken chat templates, and dropped MTP or
        vision tensors. SFT-class edits can re-refuse inside an agent harness. Lower quants can change the refusal
        boundary again — stay at Q4 and up; prefer a higher quant when you evaluate behavior. Editor quality varies.
        None of that is a reason to skip the original.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted">
        Test official Qwen3.6-35B-A3B on llama.cpp first. If the official instruct cannot finish a prompt, an edited
        checkpoint will not debug your ROCm, Vulkan, or template problem. Stock Qwen on loopback already has no cloud
        moderator — the weights can still refuse.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl">Vet the uploader and the file</h2>
      <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-muted">
        {abliteratedGuide.trust.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
        <li>Prefer the publisher’s card over a random re-quant. Pin a revision with <code class="font-mono text-ink">huggingface-cli</code> or a SHA-256, not “latest”.</li>
        <li>Read LICENSE. Qwen bases are often Apache-2.0; derivatives differ. Gated repos exist for a reason.</li>
        <li>If the card advertises MTP or vision, confirm those tensors are actually in the file you downloaded.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-faint">
        Search terms we already list: {abliteratedGuide.search_patterns.join(', ')}. No download stats.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl">Catalog</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-muted">
        Driven by <code class="font-mono text-ink">src/data/abliterated.yaml</code> as of {abliteratedGuide.as_of}.
        Never invent a row. Quants are Q4 and up only. Calculator share links exist only for the official control —
        we have cited architecture for that stack. Other rows have no cited KV dims in this catalog, so there is no
        share link (do not invent one). Tested abliterated pick: <strong class="text-ink">{testedPick}</strong>.
      </p>
      <AbliteratedHubTable />
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl">Run on an R9700 (llama.cpp)</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted">
        llama.cpp first. Vulkan is the daily-driver path on our rig. HIP is fine if <code class="font-mono text-ink">rocminfo</code> is clean
        and the device is gfx1201. Exact flags and measured VRAM for any abliterated file are
        <strong class="text-ink">TODO</strong> — we have not published a command line for those checkpoints. Use the
        official instruct tutorial, then swap only the <code class="font-mono text-ink">-m</code> path after the control works.
      </p>
      <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal space-y-2 pl-5 text-muted">
        <li>Prove <a class="underline decoration-accent/40 underline-offset-2" href="/tutorials/run-local-llm">official Qwen on llama.cpp</a> (Vulkan first).</li>
        <li>Keep GGUFs on NVMe. One LLM per GPU. Floor is Q4 — prefer Q5 when the file exists.</li>
        <li>Download the publisher file. Pin a revision. Do not take a random mirror.</li>
        <li>Swap <code class="font-mono text-ink">-m</code> to that GGUF. Leave <code class="font-mono text-ink">-ngl 99</code> as “offload what fits.” If layers spill to RAM, stop — offload kills speed.</li>
        <li>Watch termination and think-loops. Compare the same prompt on official instruct.</li>
      </ol>
      <pre class="mt-5 overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl border border-line bg-raised/40 p-4 font-mono text-[0.8rem] leading-relaxed text-muted"><code>{`# Vulkan build — same as the official tutorial
cmake -B build-vk -DGGML_VULKAN=ON -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
cmake --build build-vk -j"$(nproc)"

# HIP only if rocminfo is clean (gfx1201). Flags: see /backends — do not invent extra HIP switches.

# After official instruct works, swap the file. Exact abliterated flags: TODO.
./build-vk/bin/llama-cli \\
  -m /path/to/pinned.gguf \\
  -ngl 99 \\
  -c 8192 \\
  -p "A short smoke prompt you already ran on official instruct." \\
  -n 64`}</code></pre>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-faint">
        Context 8192 here is a conservative smoke, not a claim. The daily-driver 262K path is for official Qwen3.6-35B-A3B
        Q5 on one R9700. Open the <a class="underline decoration-accent/40 underline-offset-2" href="/calculator">calculator</a> before you copy that context onto an untested file.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl">Responsible use</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted">
        Educational local open-weight context only. These files are not a Stack Lab profile and not the weekly default.
        Abliteration and “uncensored” fine-tunes can remove refusals that the original model had. You are responsible
        for what you generate and for following the law and the model license. We do not host weights, we do not
        provide a how-to for harm, and we do not recommend these as a daily driver. If you do not need an edited
        checkpoint, stay on official instruct.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>
